        <section id="network" aria-labelledby="network-title">
          <h1 class="page-title" id="network-title">Networking</h1>
          <p class="intro">Read this when the app needs the internet, a database or your own machine. A guest has no
            network unless the site asks for one. In a page, its only way out is then the visitor's own
            browser.</p>

          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="map">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">The guest wants</th><th scope="col">What it takes</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>HTTP, HTTPS, WebSockets</td><td><a href="#fetch"><code>--network fetch</code></a>, and a server that allows CORS</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Other TCP (a database)</td><td><a href="#tunnels">a tunnel</a>, over a WebSocket in a page</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A password or token</td><td><a href="#credentials">a token endpoint</a>, never a secret in the image</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Your own machine (loopback, private addresses)</td><td><a href="#credentials"><code>--allow-private</code></a>, for builds and <code>snowglobe run</code>; never in a page</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Another machine of the daemon</td><td><a href="#machines">a link</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>UDP</td><td>not possible in a page</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 class="h3" id="fetch">HTTP, HTTPS and WebSockets</h2>
          <p>Build with <code>--network fetch</code> (or <code>snowglobe.network="fetch"</code>). The guest gets a
            network card and DHCP. Every request it makes leaves as the page's own browser request. Its
            connections to ports 80 and 443 end inside the machine:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>HTTP</strong> requests become a <code>fetch()</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>HTTPS</strong> is terminated in the machine and replayed with
              <code>fetch("https://…")</code>. The certificate is signed by a CA made fresh for each build, which the
              guest trusts. Nothing else should trust that CA: its key ships with the site.</li>
            <li><strong>WebSockets</strong> open a browser <code>WebSocket</code> to the same URL.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The browser's rules still apply:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>HTTP(S) only reaches servers that allow CORS. Others answer with a 502 that says so.</li>
            <li>Loopback, private and link-local addresses (<code>localhost</code>, 10/8, 192.168/16, 169.254/16,
              fc00::/7 and the like) are refused, so a guest can't reach the visitor's own machine or network.
              The page's own origin stays reachable.</li>
            <li>Requests carry none of the visitor's cookies, HTTP authentication or referrer: they are the guest's,
              not the visitor's.</li>
            <li>A request body or WebSocket message is at most 64 MiB.</li>
            <li>UDP goes nowhere.</li>
            <li>Other TCP goes only through a tunnel.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="h3" id="tunnels">Other TCP: tunnels</h2>
          <p>A page can't open raw TCP. So a connection to a database, or to any port but 80 and 443, is refused
            unless the site names it as a tunnel.</p>
          <p><code>--tunnel HOST:PORT=URL</code> carries every connection to <code>HOST:PORT</code> over a WebSocket
            to <code>URL</code>, as binary messages. That is what websockify, websocat and Neon's wsproxy speak.</p>
          <pre>websocat --binary ws-l:0.0.0.0:6543 tcp:127.0.0.1:5432       <span class="dim"># next to Postgres</span>
snowglobe build ./my-app --network fetch --tunnel db.internal:5432=wss://db-proxy.example.com/</pre>

          <h2 class="h3" id="credentials">Credentials</h2>
          <div class="note">
            <p>Whatever the guest knows ships with the site. A password in its environment is in the snapshot, for
              every visitor.</p>
          </div>
          <p>Use a token instead:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Put <code>{token}</code> in the tunnel URL.</li>
            <li>Name a token endpoint with <code>--tunnel-token URL</code>. The page asks it for a fresh token on
              every connection (natively, once per boot).</li>
            <li>Put a gateway behind the tunnel. It checks the token and holds the real credentials.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>A token only replaces the password. What it opens is whatever the gateway lets any visitor do.</p>
          <p>Builds and <code>snowglobe run</code> refuse loopback and private addresses, so a guest can't reach the
            host's own services. That holds through <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> or <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> too: a private IP
            or <code>localhost</code> handed to the proxy is refused. <code>--allow-private</code> lets them, for a tunnel endpoint on your own machine
            while developing.</p>

          <h2 class="h3" id="native">Outside a browser</h2>
          <p>Run natively, the network card goes out through the host instead of a page. So a tunnel's URL may also
            be <code>tcp://HOST:PORT</code>: each connection the guest opens to the tunnel's name becomes one to that
            address, with no WebSocket proxy in between. The same rules apply, so a tunnel to your own machine needs
            <code>--allow-private</code>.</p>
          <pre>snowglobe run dist/app --tunnel db.internal:5432=tcp://127.0.0.1:5432 --allow-private</pre>
          <p><code>snowglobe-vm</code>, and an executable made by <code>snowglobe bundle</code>, also take
            <code>--egress</code>:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>none</code> refuses every connection the guest opens itself. Links and forwarded ports still
              work.</li>
            <li><code>internet</code> allows public addresses.</li>
            <li><code>private</code> adds loopback and private networks, as <code>--allow-private</code> does.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="h3" id="machines">Between machines: links</h2>
          <p>Machines in the daemon reach each other only through links. A link gives a machine a name to reach
            another by: its guest connects to <code>db.internal:5432</code> and gets the target's guest port. A link
            works even when the machine's egress is <code>none</code>, and opens no TCP port on the host.</p>
          <p>Links, network modes and each template's egress are covered on
            <a href="../orchestration/#links">Orchestration and fleets</a>.</p>
        </section>
